    "text": "To find the least positive multiple of 72 that has exactly 16 positive factors, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Prime Factorization of 72**:\n   \\[\n   72 = 2^3 \\times 3^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Form of a Number with 16 Factors**:\n   The number of factors of a number \\( n \\) with prime factorization \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n   \\]\n   We need this product to equal 16. The possible factorizations of 16 are:\n   \\[\n   16 = 16 \\times 1 \\quad \\text{(i.e., \\( n = p_1^{15} \\))}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   16 = 8 \\times 2 \\quad \\text{(i.e., \\( n = p_1^7 \\times p_2^1 \\))}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   16 = 4 \\times 4 \\quad \\text{(i.e., \\( n = p_1^3 \\times p_2^3 \\))}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   16 = 4 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\quad \\text{(i.e., \\( n = p_1^3 \\times p_2^1 \\times p_3^1 \\))}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   16 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\quad \\text{(i.e., \\( n = p_1^1 \\times p_2^1 \\times p_3^1 \\times p_4^1 \\))}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Finding the Least Multiple of 72**:\n   We need to construct numbers from these factorizations that are multiples of \\( 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\) and have exactly 16 factors. We will check each form to find the smallest number.\n\n   - **Form \\( n = p_1^{15} \\)**:\n     \\[\n     2^{15} = 32768 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3^{15} = 14348907 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Form \\( n = p_1^7 \\times p_2^1 \\)**:\n     \\[\n     2^7 \\times 3 = 128 \\times 3 = 384 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2^7 \\times 5 = 128 \\times 5 = 640 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3^7 \\times 2 = 2187 \\times 2 = 4374 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Form \\( n = p_1^3 \\times p_2^3 \\)**:\n     \\[\n     2^3 \\times 3^3 = 8 \\times 27 = 216 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2^3 \\times 5^3 = 8 \\times 125 = 1000 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     3^3 \\times 7^3 = 27 \\times 343 = 9441 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Form \\( n = p_1^3 \\times p_2^1 \\times p_3^1 \\)**:\n     \\[\n     2^3 \\times 3 \\times 5 = 8 \\times 3 \\times 5 = 120 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2^3 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 8 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 168 \\quad \\text{(a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2^3 \\times 5 \\times 7 = 8 \\times 5 \\times 7 = 280 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Form \\( n = p_1^1 \\times p_2^1 \\times p_3^1 \\times p_4^1 \\)**:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 = 210 \\quad \\text{(not a multiple of 72)}\n     \\]\n\n   The smallest number among the valid candidates is 168.\n\n4. **Verification**:\n   - Prime factorization of 168:\n     \\[\n     168 = 2^3 \\times 3 \\times 7\n     \\]\n   - Number of factors:\n     \\[\n     (3+1)(1+1)(1+1) = 4 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 16\n     \\]\n\nThus, the least positive multiple of 72 that has exactly 16 positive factors is \\(\\boxed{168}\\).",
